1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,256
The person I’ve chosen to talk about is the French writer Victor Hugo –

2
00:00:06,256 --> 00:00:11,669
many people have heard of him because his novel, Les Misérables,

3
00:00:11,669 --> 00:00:16,020
which he wrote in 1862, is famous around the world.

4
00:00:16,020 --> 00:00:19,814
It became a stage musical in the 1980s,

5
00:00:19,814 --> 00:00:24,170
and a film version was also released in 2012.

6
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:29,314
So, some of us, I’m sure, have a pretty general idea of the

7
00:00:29,314 --> 00:00:30,118
plot,

8
00:00:30,118 --> 00:00:32,670
but we know much less about the author.

9
00:00:32,670 --> 00:00:38,649
Today, I’m going to provide a little more insight into this talented man

10
00:00:38,649 --> 00:00:43,383
and I’m going to talk particularly about the home he had on the island

11
00:00:43,383 --> 00:00:45,530
of Guernsey in the British Channel Islands.

12
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:52,106
But first, his early career … as I’ve said, he was a writer, he

13
00:00:52,106 --> 00:00:55,935
was at the height of his career in Paris and he was very highly

14
00:00:55,935 --> 00:00:57,860
regarded by his colleagues.

15
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:03,086
As far as literature was concerned, he was the leading figure of the Romantic

16
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:03,990
movement.

17
00:01:03,990 --> 00:01:10,476
However, as well as being a literary genius, he also gave many speeches about

18
00:01:10,476 --> 00:01:14,070
issues like the level of poverty in his society.

19
00:01:14,070 --> 00:01:20,312
He felt very strongly about this and about other areas where change was needed,

20
00:01:20,312 --> 00:01:22,560
like education.

21
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:28,129
This kind of outspoken criticism was not well liked by the rulers of France

22
00:01:28,129 --> 00:01:34,600
and, eventually, the emperor – Napoleon III – told Victor Hugo to leave Paris

23
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:35,802
and not return;

24
00:01:35,802 --> 00:01:40,410
in other words, he sent him into exile.

25
00:01:40,410 --> 00:01:46,270
So Victor Hugo was forced to reside in other parts of Europe.

26
00:01:46,270 --> 00:01:53,410
Guernsey was actually his third place of exile and he landed there in 1855.

27
00:01:53,410 --> 00:01:59,045
He produced a lot while on Guernsey – including Les Misérables –

28
00:01:59,045 --> 00:02:03,175
and to do this, he had to spend a great deal of time in

29
00:02:03,175 --> 00:02:04,920
the home that he had there.

30
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:10,086
This was a property that he bought using the money he’d made in France

31
00:02:10,086 --> 00:02:12,840
from the publication of a collection of his poetry.

32
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:16,150
It was the only property he ever owned,

33
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:22,350
and he was very proud of it.

34
00:02:22,350 --> 00:02:28,501
The property Victor Hugo bought on Guernsey was a large, five-storey house in the

35
00:02:28,501 --> 00:02:34,591
capital town of St Peter Port and he lived there for 15 years, returning

36
00:02:34,591 --> 00:02:39,480
to France in 1870 when Napoleon’s Empire collapsed.

37
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:42,489
He decorated and furnished each level,

38
00:02:42,489 --> 00:02:46,562
or floor, of the house in unique and wonderful ways,

39
00:02:46,562 --> 00:02:51,497
and many people consider the inside of the house to be a ‘work of

40
00:02:51,497 --> 00:02:52,400
art’.

41
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:59,930
Today it’s a museum that attracts 200,000 visitors a year.

42
00:02:59,930 --> 00:03:03,981
He lived in the house with his family … and portraits of its members

43
00:03:03,981 --> 00:03:09,096
still hang in rooms on the ground floor, along with drawings that he did

44
00:03:09,096 --> 00:03:14,030
during his travels that he felt were important to him.

45
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:20,183
In other ground-floor rooms, there are huge tapestries that he would have designed

46
00:03:20,183 --> 00:03:21,490
and loved.

47
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:27,851
The walls are covered in dark wood panelling that Victor Hugo created himself using

48
00:03:27,851 --> 00:03:30,760
wooden furniture that he bought in the market.

49
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:34,159
The items were relatively inexpensive,

50
00:03:34,159 --> 00:03:38,020
and he used them to create intricate carvings.

51
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:44,370
They gave an atmosphere on the lower level that was shadowy and rather solemn.

52
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:50,453
On the next level of the house there are two impressive lounges, where he

53
00:03:50,453 --> 00:03:52,200
entertained his guests.

54
00:03:52,200 --> 00:03:59,470
One lounge has entirely red furnishings, such as sofas and wall coverings,

55
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:01,900
and the other blue.

56
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:08,796
There’s a strong Chinese influence in these areas in things like the wallpaper pattern

57
00:04:08,796 --> 00:04:15,230
and the lamps – which he would have made himself by copying original versions.

58
00:04:15,230 --> 00:04:22,593
His library, where he left many of his favourite books, forms the hallway to the third floor

59
00:04:22,593 --> 00:04:28,210
and was a comfortable area where he could relax and enjoy his afternoons.

60
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:35,500
And then, at the very top of the house, there’s a room called the Lookout – called that

61
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:37,950
because it looks out over the harbour.

62
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:43,990
In contrast to the rather dark lower levels, it’s full of light

63
00:04:43,990 --> 00:04:49,167
and was like a glass office where he would write until lunchtime – often

64
00:04:49,167 --> 00:04:50,130
at his desk.

65
00:04:50,130 --> 00:04:54,567
So, Victor Hugo was a man of many talents,

66
00:04:54,567 --> 00:04:58,000
but he was also true to his values.

67
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:03,832
While living in his house on Guernsey, he entertained many other famous writers,

68
00:05:03,832 --> 00:05:09,744
but he also invited a large group of local children from the deprived areas

69
00:05:09,744 --> 00:05:12,510
of the island to dinner once a week.

70
00:05:12,510 --> 00:05:16,787
What’s more, he served them their food,

71
00:05:16,787 --> 00:05:20,240
which was an extraordinary gesture for the time period.

72
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:25,557
In 1927, the house was owned by his relatives,

73
00:05:25,557 --> 00:05:29,710
and they decided to donate it to the city of Paris.

74
00:05:29,710 --> 00:05:34,802
It has since been restored using photographs from the period

75
00:05:34,802 --> 00:05:40,657
and, as I mentioned earlier, is now a museum that is open to the

76
00:05:40,657 --> 00:05:41,940
public.
